---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/PilumRTS/pilum-releases";
// Direct installer URLs from the latest release's manifests; the script at the bottom refreshes them after a new release.
async function installer(os: string) {
  const manifest = await (await fetch(`${repo}/releases/latest/download/release-manifest-${os}.json`)).json();
  return `${repo}/releases/download/v${manifest.version}/${manifest.installer}`;
}
const mac = await installer("mac");
const windows = await installer("windows");
const youtube = "https://www.youtube.com/@PilumRTS";
const discord = "https://discord.gg/pv4KJVRcn3";
const email = "info@pilumrts.com";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---

<Layout
  title="Pilum: frontier colony RTS for Mac and Windows"
  description="Pump water, mine the rock, take what you can hold. Raiders come at night. Pilum is a tactical RTS of frontier colonies and war, in early alpha for Mac and Windows."
>
  <header class="topbar">
    <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Pilum, back to top">
      <span class="brand-mark" style={`--mask: url(${base}helmet-mask.png)`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="brand-name">PILUM</span>
    </a>
    <nav aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#about">About</a>
      <a href="#gameplay">Gameplay</a>
      <a href="#download">Download</a>
      <a href="#follow">Follow</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main id="top">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="helmet" style={`--mask: url(${base}helmet-mask.png)`} aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <p class="kicker"><span class="tag">Early alpha</span> Build by day. Fight by night.</p>
        <h1 class="logo">PILUM</h1>
        <p class="headline">Every well you dig is a well you'll have to defend.</p>
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#download">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0-5-5m5 5 5-5M4 20h16" /></svg>
            Download the alpha
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={youtube} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 5v14l11-7z" class="fill" /></svg>
            Watch on YouTube
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={discord} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Join the Discord
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="platforms">Mac and Windows</p>
      </div>
      <div class="horizon" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </section>

    <section id="about" class="section about">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="label"><span>01</span> The frontier</p>
        <div class="copy">
          <p class="lead">
            Pilum is a tactical RTS of frontier colonies and war. You're the commander sent to the edge: a landing pad,
            a dry plain, and settlers with nowhere else to go.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pump water from below the dust. Mine the rock for what it's worth. Trade with other outposts, and trust them
            as far as their guns reach. Every newcomer is a citizen, a worker or a rifle. You choose which, and you live
            with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The raiders come at dusk. Rival outposts want your water, your minerals and the buildings you raised. Beyond
            the rim, something worse is watching.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing out here goes to waste. Knock out an enemy vehicle and it's yours to crew; lose one and it's theirs.
            Take a building without levelling it, move your own citizens in, and man it against the people who built it.
            Whatever you leave standing can change hands.
          </p>
          <p>Hold long enough and your forward operating base becomes a starport town. Then they send you past the rim.</p>
          <p class="note">Early alpha: features and visuals may change.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="gameplay" class="section gameplay">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="label"><span>02</span> Gameplay</p>
        <h2>Field manual</h2>
        <div class="features">
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>The soldiers</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Every soldier is one man with his own brain: he finds his own cover, returns fire, suppresses, and runs out of breath and ammunition.</li>
              <li>He hears gunfire before he sees the shooter. Spotting takes time; a man lying still is seen later than one running across the open.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Four squads</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Assault</strong>, ten rifles with frags, smoke, C4 charges and grappling hooks.</li>
              <li><strong>Support</strong>, three men with a heavy machine gun, AP mines and a sentry gun.</li>
              <li><strong>Engineers</strong>, three men with AT rockets, AT mines and the tools to put a broken tank back on its tracks.</li>
              <li><strong>Recon</strong>, three snipers with a drone, motion trackers and a cloak that turns them into a shimmer in the air.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Wounds and loot</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Wounds land where the round did: a hit arm spoils his aim and his throw, one bad leg and he can't sprint.</li>
              <li>Low on supplies? The dead don't need theirs. Soldiers strip the fallen of their ammo and supplies. The enemy loots yours.</li>
              <li>A killing hit knocks a hull out. Some burn, most lie there for whichever side's engineers reach them first, to repair and crew.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Breach and clear</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>The squad stacks on both sides of the door, opens it, smashes it or blows it, and then flows in.</li>
              <li>Frags go over walls and blow men off roofs and ladders.</li>
              <li>Smoke blinds both sides for half a minute. It isn't cover: rounds go straight through.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Heavy weapons</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Machine-gun nests, mortar pits and wheeled mortars, crewed by whoever gets there first.</li>
              <li>Heavy weapons operate at full speed only when every seat is filled.</li>
              <li>Sentry guns, anti-tank rockets, and mines the enemy never sees until they go off.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Armour</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Battle tanks, wheeled and tracked APCs, technicals, jeeps and trucks.</li>
              <li>Every seat has its job: the driver sees ahead, the gunner along his barrel, the vehicle commander all round, head out of the hatch where a sniper can reach him.</li>
              <li>Tanks run men down, ram through walls and shove wrecks aside. Shoot off a track and it stays where it is until engineers get to it.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Nothing stays standing</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Walls break into torn ends and rubble. Take out enough of them and the house comes down, roof and all, on whoever is inside.</li>
              <li>Bridges can be blown with C4 charges.</li>
              <li>Shells crater the ground and crack asphalt to gravel. Trees fall and power lines come down.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Day and night</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>A ten-minute day: build in the light, watch the edge of the map at dusk for raiders.</li>
              <li>Burning wrecks light the street. Headlights throw the soldiers' shadows.</li>
              <li>The map is black until you scout it, and grey wherever your men can't see. Lose sight of an enemy and all you keep is where he was.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>The colony</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Gather water, ore and building materials. Grow food, manufacture munitions, manage money. Keep the power on and a roof over every head.</li>
              <li>Settlers arrive when the place is worth it and leave when the water runs out.</li>
              <li>Soldiers need food, supplies and steady pay. Run short, and you'll learn what loyalty costs.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Raids, rivals and capture</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>As your colony grows it will attract more raiders. They go for your warehouses and your supplies.</li>
              <li>Rival outposts play by your rules: warlords, raiders, expanders, stewards and partners.</li>
              <li>Take enemy buildings standing: clear them, hold them, and they're yours with everything inside. They work once your own workers move in. Rivals can do the same to you.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>Missions</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Last man standing. Body count. Eliminate the commander.</li>
              <li>Capture and hold: up to three flags, three minutes to keep them.</li>
              <li>Conquer. Sabotage: C4 on their arsenal. Retrieve: the intel case, and the man carrying it can only walk.</li>
              <li>In build mode: win by domination, by population or by wealth.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card feature">
            <h3>With friends, or against them</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Play solo or with friends against the AI, or against each other: up to four sides in every mission.</li>
              <li>Procedurally generated maps from tiny to huge: rivers with bridges and fords, houses and walled compounds you fight through room by room.</li>
              <li>Write your own AI commander in any language, or plug in an AI agent.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Early alpha: features and visuals may change.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="download" class="section download">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="label"><span>03</span> Download</p>
        <h2>Ship out</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Mac</h3>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href={mac} data-download="mac">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0-5-5m5 5 5-5M4 20h16" /></svg>
              Download for Mac
            </a>
            <p class="small">
              The Mac build isn't notarized yet. On first launch, open System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security and
              click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Windows</h3>
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href={windows} data-download="windows">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0-5-5m5 5 5-5M4 20h16" /></svg>
              Download for Windows
            </a>
            <p class="small">
              SmartScreen may say “Windows protected your PC”. Click <strong>More info</strong> →
              <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="follow" class="section follow">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="label"><span>04</span> Follow</p>
        <h2>Stay on the line</h2>
        <ul class="links">
          <li>
            <a href={youtube} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <span class="link-kind">YouTube</span>
              <span class="link-value">@PilumRTS</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={discord} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <span class="link-kind">Discord</span>
              <span class="link-value">Pilum RTS</span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>
              <span class="link-kind">Contact</span>
              <span class="link-value">{email}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="footer">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span>© 2026 Pilum.</span>
      <span>Early alpha.</span>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    fetch("https://api.github.com/repos/PilumRTS/pilum-releases/releases/latest")
      .then((response) => response.json())
      .then((release) => {
        for (const asset of release.assets) {
          const os = asset.name.endsWith(".dmg") ? "mac" : asset.name.endsWith("-setup.exe") ? "windows" : null;
          if (!os) continue;
          for (const link of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>(`[data-download="${os}"]`)) {
            link.href = asset.browser_download_url;
          }
        }
      })
      .catch(() => {});
  </script>
</Layout>
